&lt;p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:0in;margin-right:-.25in;margin-bottom:
0in;margin-left:0in;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
40.5pt'>&lt;span style='font-size:12.0pt'>We all believe that &lt;b>(1)&lt;/b> salvation
means being brought back into personal relationship with God (&lt;u>reconciliation&lt;/u>).
In this respect, 2 Corinthians&lt;b> 5&lt;/b>:17-21 stands out by using &lt;u>reconciliation&lt;/u>
repeatedly to describe the nature and purpose of Christian mission: &lt;i>“ . . .
in Christ God was reconciling the world to himself . . . having given us the
ministry of reconciliation”&lt;/i> (&lt;b>5&lt;/b>:19).&lt;span style='position:relative;
top:-3.0pt'>1&lt;/span> &lt;/span>&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>SIX NOTATIONS ON IDENTIFICATION ATONEMENT THEORY</title><link>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/atonement/six-notations-on-identification-atonement-theory/</link><pubDate>Thu, 02 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/atonement/six-notations-on-identification-atonement-theory/</guid><description>&lt;style>
