&lt;/style>
&lt;div class="word-content">
&lt;p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:-.5in;text-align:justify'>&lt;span
style='font-size:12.0pt'>            Since the Reformation, a common understanding
of Christ’s atonement work considers his righteousness as a substitute for ours
and his death as a substitute for our death as punishment for our sin. The
comments below shoulder a negative burden of proof to show that the penal
substitution understanding is foreign to atonement theory unless we adopt the
rest of Calvin’s legal theological method, where penal substitution &lt;u>would&lt;/u>
make sense. The thesis here is that substitutionary atonement theory does not
belong in non-non-legal theology.&lt;/span>&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>STRICTURES ON THE PENAL SUBSTITUTIONARY ATONEMENT THEORY: IDENTIFICATION AS AN ALTERNATIVE PROPOSAL</title><link>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/atonement/strictures-on-the-penal-substitutionary-atonement-theory-identification-as-an/</link><pubDate>Thu, 02 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/atonement/strictures-on-the-penal-substitutionary-atonement-theory-identification-as-an/</guid><description>&lt;style>
